# 279 · A runner on macOS itself, for MLX and Metal work

**Status:** `PROPOSED` · **Version:** [Later](../roadmap.md#later), until the owner gives it one · **Tracked in:** [GitHub issue 93](https://github.com/iammrduncan/thermite/issues/93), the source of truth for this work. This file keeps its context.

A proposal, split from goal [106](106-apple-silicon-rig.goal) when that goal was built. Nothing of it is built.

## What

A runner that runs on macOS itself, on a Mac with Apple silicon, so that a campaign's work can use Metal directly: MLX work first of all.

The owner, 2026-11-13, wanted a rig that can do MLX and Metal work on a Mac.

### What Thermite does today

- A rig on a Mac uses the Mac's GPU as a Vulkan device inside its Linux container (goal [106](106-apple-silicon-rig.goal), the target `vulkan-gpu`): Mesa's Venus driver in the container, the Mac's Metal behind it. That serves engines with a Vulkan back end. In the one measurement made, llama.cpp's Vulkan back end ran there at 76 to 77% of its Metal back end's speed on macOS itself.
- It does not serve MLX. MLX needs Metal, and no Linux container has Metal: a container on macOS runs in a Linux virtual machine. Goal 106's study read the other ways too (Apple's `container` tool, a program on the Mac that containers ask); none gives a kit's own engine Metal inside a wall.

### What this goal delivers

1. **A runner process on macOS,** beside a core that may stay in a container. A runner is already apart from its core (goal [206](_completed/206-core-and-runners.goal)); this one is a program on the Mac, not a container.
2. **A fence by macOS's own mechanisms,** with the same promises as on Linux: the agents can't read the judge, its kits, the record or secrets, and can't change a measurement. An isolation self-test that proves each one.
3. **A target for Metal,** with what macOS reports of the GPU and its memory, and a smoke kit on MLX or Metal.
4. **Setup** from the one-machine install, with the same checks.

### Open questions

- What the wall is on macOS (users and groups, the sandbox, a virtual machine of macOS), and whether it can be tested in CI.
- Whether this is one piece of work with goal [277](277-runner-without-a-container-engine.goal), the runner with no container engine to set up, which the owner put in v0.4.0: both need a wall that is not a Linux container.
- Whether the judge on such a runner times with the GPU idle for the agents, as a rig with one GPU does today.
- How much of a Mac's unified memory a run may take. Goal 106 found that the Vulkan device reports the whole memory and no use; macOS itself reports more.

## Why

- The owner's wish above, and of 2026-10-23 on the one-machine install: a machine that is a Mac should give its rig the Mac's unified memory and its Apple silicon GPU, for MLX and work like it.
- Goal 106 gives a Mac's GPU to engines with a Vulkan back end only, at about three quarters of Metal's speed. MLX is the framework made for Apple silicon, and it is what the owner named.

## Constraints

- **Agents never judge.** The fence on macOS keeps the same promises as the one on Linux, and the self-test proves them before a run starts.
- **No default caps.**
- A study of the wall comes first; no code is written before the owner agrees it.

## Done when

- [ ] Written when the goal is accepted into a version.

## Progress

- 2026-11-13: proposed, when goal 106 was built on the Vulkan way and left MLX apart.

